Ladybird’s Notification.requestPermission() refuses to prompt from a
document whose origin isn’t its top-level origin, and without transient
activation, which it consumes when it does prompt; a refused request
resolves with the current state (“default” when nothing is stored), and
so does a dismissed prompt. A document that isn’t a secure context
already reads “denied” from the Permissions API’s “permission state”
algorithm, so its request resolves “denied” at once, with no message.
WebKit’s Notification::requestPermission() denies the frame, insecure,
and no-gesture cases with console errors, consuming the activation
through DOMWindow::consumeTransientActivation(), and resolves “denied”
for them. Blink’s Notification::requestPermission() only reports a
discouraged-API violation for a missing gesture and counts the
cross-origin-iframe and insecure cases, leaving the refusals to the
browser’s NotificationPermissionContext. Gecko’s
NotificationPermissionRequest::Run() auto-denies without a gesture
under dom.webnotifications.requireuserinteraction, resolving with the
raw stored state (“default”) and a console error. All three resolve
“default” for a prompt the user dismissed.
